  "surprise": "Discard: 616/663/664 (exact mean 647.67) vs 153-claude's 676/700/685 (687.00): -39.33, paired -60/-37/-21, every seed down and below the registered -35..+35 range. No other keep landed during the run (155-claude's low-rank bigram was discarded at 668), so the incumbent stays 153. Mechanism confirmed, effect reversed, as with 152's drop-path. The curriculum ran as designed and was free: 1,132 batches per epoch, steps 4448/4425/4496 (153: 4540/4479/4489, -1.5%, inside the registered 4,200-4,800; the < 4,000 falsifier did not fire), 3.93/3.91/3.97 epochs. The all-lengths val curve shows the ascending epoch exactly: 0.277/0.266/0.259 at 30 s, RISING to 0.546/0.454/0.454 at 75 s while train CE fell to ~0.05 (the short-proof solution is actively wrong for long proofs), then 0.125/0.098/0.091 at 90 s once the 6-line batches arrived (epoch 1 ends near step 1,150) and inside 153's band by 120 s (0.092/0.078/0.086, as predicted). Endpoint fit is indistinguishable from the incumbent's: final val 0.0850/0.0864/0.0896 (153: 0.088/0.087/0.089; minima 0.0788/0.0784/0.0805 at 195/120/270 s), train 0.055/0.054/0.060, held-out greedy after pretraining 0.960/0.960/0.970 (153: 0.9675/0.967/0.968). Yet the handed-off model was a much worse round-1 sampler: targets solved 634/247/590 (153: 766/747/694; the registered '< 550 on two seeds' falsifier fired on one), sample acc 0.238/0.073/0.217 (153: 0.271/0.28/0.21), both-ok samples 9403/2886/8631 of 48,000, round-1 Lean parse failures 22.9/48.2/27.9% (153: 30.6/30.5/41.0%, so on seeds 0/2 the samples parsed but were wrong proofs; seed 1 also degenerated syntactically). Expert iteration climbed faster than the incumbent, +444/+838/+560 over rounds 1-4 to 1078/1085/1150 (153: +391/+421/+497 to 1157/1168/1191; 148's bad-start-fast-climb pattern), but never caught up: round-4 sample acc 0.677/0.669/0.723 (153 s0: 0.729), eval/round-4 transfer 0.571/0.611/0.577 (153: 0.584/0.599/0.575, unchanged), >=10 87/98/103 (96.0 vs 107.7), >=11 16/20/20 (18.7 vs 23.7), >=12 4/4/4, L* 11/11/11 (153: 11/12/11), final held-out greedy 0.9885/0.989/0.9955; totals 1663/1572/1722 s, RL sampling 238-302 samples/s. Reading: the order of the first epoch leaves a mark that three shuffled epochs and an identical final loss do not erase. The curriculum model memorizes the cap-6 set equally well but samples worse at T=0.8 on length >= 7 targets from round 1 on, and here the metric followed the RL start (as in 153's keep; 152 is the counterexample where a better start lost). Two candidate mechanisms, not separable in this run: (a) the peak-LR phase (warmup plus ~1,150 steps at lr 1e-3) was spent on 2-3-line proofs, so the features long compositions need were fitted only at lower LR into a basin chosen by short proofs (the 0.45-0.55 val spike at 75 s shows that basin is wrong for long proofs); (b) under Muon momentum 0.95 and Adam moments the non-stationary epoch is simply a worse optimizer trajectory. Either way 'short proofs first' points the wrong way for a metric that lives beyond the training lengths; the untested follow-ups in this lane are the reverse order (long first) or an ascending LAST epoch so the handoff is fresh on 6-line proofs, and this run gives no evidence on them. Clean run: all seeds ok, registration d10c486 committed before pods, run_idea.py exit 0, pods finished in 32-33 min and were terminated (pod list shows no 157-fable3 pods). Candidate added 16 lines and deleted 6, all reverted.",
